<%# FREE BELOW ZERO — THE SIGNAL (computed-claims spec §4). Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    Split out of `_trouble` because the strip's other three arms are one element each and this one is
    a figure, a list and a pace — inlined, the `case` that dispatches the four kinds would be
    unreadable and a fifth kind would be added below the wrong `end`.

    ** THE STRIP MIRRORS THE HERO (two-shapes spec §2). ** It said "Your rules claim more than you
    have" on `#short?` alone while the hero was splitting that same negative three ways, so the two
    panels could contradict each other on the same afternoon and did. `free = pot − Σ claims` has
    ONE cause per sign now, so both panels say the same thing for the same reason:

      free < 0  → "Your rules claim $X more than checking holds", the give-way walk, the remainder
                  if the shortfall outlasts it, the pace, and — where another account holds money —
                  "Move some in from your other accounts."

    THE WALK IS THE PRESENTER'S (`#uncovered_claims`), not this file's, and it is gated there on the
    same `#short?` this strip renders under. It takes each claim in turn until the shortfall is
    absorbed, so the LAST row is usually only PARTLY uncovered — "Car repair is short $120" is a
    different sentence from "Car repair is uncovered", and only the walk can tell them apart.

    THE PACE IS SAID ON BOTH ARMS, deliberately: spending less for the rest of the period lands the
    figure at zero whichever way it got there, which is the one remedy §4 names that does not depend
    on which cause it was.

    ** NO FIX BUTTON, AND THE DOOR IS THE BUDGET PAGE. ** §4: "The remedy is spending less for the
    rest of the period or editing/adjusting a rule." There is nothing to move money FROM — no claim is
    money sitting anywhere — so a button offering to take $300 from Rent would be proposing an act the
    app no longer has. %>
<div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0" data-shortfall>
  <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
    <%# THE HEADLINE IS THE CAUSE, AND THE BRANCH IS A PRESENTER PREDICATE. With nothing claimed at
        all the shortfall is a plain overspend and "your rules claim more" would be false — Σ claims
        is zero — so the second arm says what actually happened. %>
    <span class="font-medium text-gray-900" data-shortfall-headline>
      <% if presenter.anything_claimed? %>
        Your rules claim <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %> more than checking holds
      <% else %>
        You have spent past what you had
      <% end %>
    </span>
    <span class="text-sm font-medium text-status-danger tabular-nums" data-shortfall-amount>
      short <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %>
    </span>
  </div>

  <%# ** THE HERO'S SECOND CLAUSE, SAID AS AN INSTRUCTION (§2). ** Money in another account is not
      subtracted from `free` and never was money in checking, so it is not an explanation of the
      shortfall — it is the thing that can close it without spending less. GATED, because it is
      false for a user whose other accounts are empty or who has none: that user reads the headline's
      own second arm ("You have spent past what you had") and this line does not render.

      THE FIGURE IS `#other_accounts_total`, THE SAME SUM THE ACCOUNTS LINE PRINTS (answers-first §6),
      so the strip cannot claim money the line below it shows as absent. It names no account, because
      the total spans all of them and "it is in savings" is a guess this panel cannot make. %>
  <% if presenter.money_parked_elsewhere? %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700" data-shortfall-elsewhere>
      <%= number_to_currency(presenter.other_accounts_total) %> of your money is sitting outside
      checking — move some in from your other accounts.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE PACE. Nil for a user who has declared no period — there is no "rest of the period" to
      spread a shortfall over — and the strip simply says one sentence fewer there.

      ** IT IS `HomeHelper#pace_words` NOW, AND THE RUNWAY SAYS THE SAME SENTENCE (two-shapes §3). **
      The runway's pace line and this remedy are one sentence about one figure; written out in two
      views they were two chances to round it differently or to reword one of them. `#pace_line`
      answers WHICH arm — and this strip only ever renders while `free` is below zero, so it is
      always the second one, at the same `#per_day_pace` figure it printed before. %>
  <% if pace = presenter.pace_line %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700" data-shortfall-pace><%= pace_words(pace) %></p>
  <% end %>

  <%# WHO GIVES WAY, AND IN THE ORDER THE WALK ACTUALLY USES (rules-own-the-budget spec §3;
      `HomePresenter#give_way_key`). It said "Reverse priority" and never mentioned the TYPE, which
      is the term the walk sorts on FIRST: every `choice` rule in the app gives way before any
      `usage` rule, and a `bill` is the last thing reached. Within a type it is the highest priority
      NUMBER first — the reverse of fill order, the lowest number being the one that keeps its money
      — and rules inside one category break on `Category.rule_order`. The same three words the
      Budget page's give-way list, its ▲▼ buttons and the category form all say.

      The figure is what THIS claim is short by after everything below it in the order has already
      given way — which is why the last row in the list is usually partial and says so. Empty on the
      two arms above that have nothing uncovered, so this renders nothing there rather than being
      gated a second way. %>
  <% if presenter.uncovered_claims.any? %>
    <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1" data-uncovered>
      <% presenter.uncovered_claims.each do |uncovered| %>
        <li class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4 text-sm text-gray-600"
            data-uncovered-claim="<%= uncovered.category.name %>">
          <span><%= uncovered.category.name %> · <%= pool_rule_label(uncovered.line.rule) %></span>
          <span class="tabular-nums">
            <% if uncovered.whole? %>
              nothing covers its <%= number_to_currency(uncovered.claim) %>
            <% else %>
              short <%= number_to_currency(uncovered.amount) %> of <%= number_to_currency(uncovered.claim) %>
            <% end %>
          </span>
        </li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>

    <%# ** THE PART OF THE SHORTFALL NO CLAIM ACCOUNTS FOR (fix round 1 — LOW-1). ** The walk stops
        when it runs out of claims, so a user who has spent further than their rules ever asked for
        read a headline of $900 over a list totalling $500. The list plus this line is the headline,
        by construction. %>
    <% if presenter.uncovered_remainder.positive? %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600" data-uncovered-remainder>
        …and <%= number_to_currency(presenter.uncovered_remainder) %> past everything the rules claim.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    <%= link_to "Change a rule on the Budget page", budget_page_path, class: "text-status-info hover:underline" %>
    if this period's plan is the thing that has to give.
  </p>
</div>
